# Investment Philosophy

What this note is for: the agent reads it to know what you want to own, what you refuse to own, and where your edge is. Be concrete. Vague principles produce vague verdicts.

## Default framework (replace with your own)

- Buy good businesses at sensible prices and let them compound; avoid permanent loss of capital over chasing the last bit of upside.
- Stay inside a circle of competence: only own what you can explain simply, including why the advantage persists.
- Demand a margin of safety between price and a conservatively estimated value.
- Think like an owner with a multi-year horizon; treat the market as a counterparty, not an oracle.
- An edge comes from one of three places: information, analysis, or behavior. Default assumption is that your durable edge is behavioral (temperament and time horizon), not informational.

> FILL THIS IN — your actual philosophy:
> - What kinds of businesses do you want to own? (quality compounders, deep value, special situations, secular-growth, etc.)
> - What is your real edge, honestly? Why do you win where others lose?
> - What is your circle of competence — which sectors/models do you genuinely understand, and which are off-limits?
> - What is your time horizon, and what return are you actually trying to earn?
> - What do you refuse to own no matter how cheap? (e.g. businesses you can't model, frauds-by-smell, things outside your circle)
> - What has historically cost you money, and what rule now prevents a repeat?
